# API route conventions

Every route handler under `app/api/v1/` should follow the pattern already
established in `lib/server/api/auth-context.ts`, not reinvent it per-route.

## Error handling

- Wrap the whole handler body in `try { ... } catch (error) { return jsonError(error); }`.
  `jsonError` already maps `ApiError` instances to their status code and logs +
  500s anything else — this keeps every endpoint's error shape consistent
  (`{ error: string }`), even for routes that "probably won't throw" today.
- Throw `ApiError(status, message)` for expected failures (missing auth, not
  found, bad input) instead of hand-rolling `NextResponse.json({ error }, { status })`
  in multiple places.
- Parse request bodies with `readJsonBody<T>(request)` (in `auth-context.ts`),
  not raw `request.json()` — malformed JSON should be a 400, not an
  unhandled `SyntaxError` that falls through to a generic 500.

## Auth-scoped data

- Any query filtered by "the current user" must use the `uid` from
  `requireFirebaseClaims(request)` (the verified token), never a client-supplied
  id/uid in the body or query string.

## Input validation

- Validate types and enforce reasonable length caps on any user-writable
  field before it reaches D1 (see the `displayName` cap in
  `app/api/v1/me/route.ts` for the pattern). Don't rely on D1 to reject bad
  input — nothing stops an unbounded string today.
- Locale params go through the existing `isLocale()`/`resolveCatalogLocale()`
  allowlist — never pass a raw query param straight into a lookup.
